## Why you can trust this data

- **Checked against a second source.** Every run reads each store's full product list, then checks one product from every store against a second source on the same store. Latest run: {{ q.js_match }} of {{ q.js_checked }} checked products matched exactly.
- **Real US prices.** Every request asks for US prices, and every run proves that the store showed US dollars. Latest run: {{ q.usd_stores }} of {{ q.stores_total }} stores proved US dollars.
- **Doubtful data is held back, not guessed.** A row that breaks a rule (for example a sale price above the normal price) is set aside, and a run that looks wrong as a whole is held: yesterday's good data stays live until a clean run replaces it. Latest run: {{ q.rows_rejected }} rows set aside, {{ "{:,}".format(q.free_variants) }} free gifts and samples left out on purpose, {{ "held" if q.held else "not held" }}.
- **Nothing silently missing.** A store that could not be read keeps its last good prices and is marked as not updated, so you always know how fresh each row is. Latest run: {{ q.stores_ok }} of {{ q.stores_total }} stores updated{% if q.not_updated %}; {{ q.not_updated|length }} kept their previous prices{% endif %}.
- **Collected politely.** One store page every few seconds, the stores' own rules respected, and the run slows down or stops as soon as a store asks. Latest run: {{ "{:,}".format(q.requests) }} page requests in {{ q.duration_min }} minutes, and {% if q.blocked %}{{ q.blocked }} store{{ "s" if q.blocked != 1 else "" }} blocked us (their last good prices stayed live){% else %}no store blocked us{% endif %}.

## How it works

<p align="center"><img src="assets/pipeline.svg" width="100%" alt="Five steps: read public store catalogs, collect politely once a day, check every price against a second source, keep the full history, deliver CSV, a monthly data file, a Google Sheet and a dashboard."></p>

1. **Collect.** Once a day, a scheduled job reads the public product catalog of each store, slowly and with the stores' rules respected.
2. **Check.** Each price is checked for obvious errors (above zero, sale price below the "was" price), and one product per store is compared with a second source.
3. **Store.** Every change is kept with the time it happened, so you can see what any product cost on any day.
4. **Deliver.** The latest prices as CSV, a daily change log, a monthly history file, an optional Google Sheet and a [live dashboard]({{ pub.dashboard }}).

<p align="center"><img src="assets/dashboard.png" width="100%" alt="The live dashboard: stores tracked, prices live, share in stock and on sale, recent changes and run history."></p>

## What broke and how I fixed it

<p align="center"><img src="assets/maintenance-loop.svg" width="100%" alt="Example timeline of daily runs. One real incident: Shopify asked the whole run to slow down; the fix pauses every store together. Daily runs delivered so far: {{ f.status.runs_good }}."></p>

Real problems from building this monitor, with the fix for each. The full write-ups are in [docs/incidents.md](docs/incidents.md).

- **Too fast for a shared host.** Each store was read slowly, but all these stores run on Shopify's servers, and together the requests were too fast. Fix: one pace for the whole run, plus an automatic stop if several stores push back at once.
- **Free gifts looked like errors.** Free samples have a price of zero, and the checks treated them as broken data and held a whole run. Fix: free items are now left out on purpose and counted separately.
- **"Slow down" was obeyed by one store only.** When Shopify asked us to wait a minute, only that store waited while the others carried on. Fix: when Shopify asks, every store pauses together, and the pace eases back only after a run of clean answers.
- **Too many test runs in one night.** After many test runs in a few hours, Shopify refused us for a while. Fix: the run now stops politely instead of retrying, the store list was trimmed so a day's run stays short, and test runs are rationed.

## Who uses data like this

- **Store owners and pricing managers:** see the day a competitor starts a sale, how deep it goes, and when it ends, without checking sites by hand.
- **Brand and category managers:** follow price levels, sale depth and stock across a whole category, store by store.
- **Analysts:** a clean daily history of prices and stock for trend reports, such as how deep Black Friday discounts go by category.

